  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Mainly dry, tranquil and cooler weather pattern \nexpected into the last weekend of July. \n\nThe airmass is drying out across CPA with sfc dewpoints\ngradually falling by several degrees from earlier this morning.\n16Z TDs range from the low 50s across the NW Mtns to the low 60s\nnear the I-99/RT 220 corridor and mid to upper 60s in the far SE\npart of the CWA. \n\na brief period of clearing skies followed closely by 2-3 deg C \nof cooling (centered around 700 mb) have helped to boost the\nareal coverage and depth of the cu field across the region with\nthe greatest sky coverage found across the Alleghenies where\neven some narrow bands of LER showers were forming over and\nstreaming SE of Lake Erie as a result of the water to 850 mb\ndelta T of around 16 deg C. \n\nExpect some brief showers across the NW Mtns this afternoon.\nHowever, a few unlucky locations that get stuck under one of\nthese 2-3NM wide bands of rain could see up to one tenth of an\ninch of rain.  \n\nPrevious Disc...\n\nCool cyclonic/WNW flow aloft will trigger light rain shower \nactivity today downwind of Lake Erie over the Allegheny \nPlateau. Hires guidance shows low clouds/stratocu slow to erode\nthis morning over the western high terrain. Exiting cold front \ncould trigger an isolated PM t-storm over the far southeast part\nof the CWA, but precip odds are less than 20%. SPC has shifted \nthe level 1/5 MRGL risk out of Lancaster County to the I95 \ncorridor. \n\nMax temps in the mid 60s to mid 70s over the Alleghenies are 5 \nto 15 degrees below the historical average for late July. The \nrelatively cool daytime temps today will be enhanced by a \nnoticeable WNW breeze gusting 20-25 mph. Mainly clear skies \nexpected tonight with below climo to chilly min temps for this \ntime of year fcst in the 40-60F range from NW to SE. Areas of \nvalley fog are expected late tonight into early Thursday \nmorning. \n\nThe cooler/less humid airmass will usher in a dry spell into the\nlast weekend of July with high pressure in control. Upper trough\ncarving out over the eastern Great Lakes into the Northeast US\nwill bring rain chances back into the forecast Sunday into next\nweek.",
